<br>

<p>For a more comprehensive list of good C++ books, you may wish to check <=
a href=3D"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/388242/the-definitive-c-book-g=
uide-and-list" title=3D"this thread" target=3D"_blank" style=3D"color: #00b=
ceb;">this thread</a> at <i>stackoverflow.com</i>.</p>

<p>If you know any other good books worth recommending, please feel free to=
 contribute to <a href=3D"https://www.facebook.com/CppInstitute/photos/a.62=
4438340916602.1073741825.478483122178792/747492841944484/?type=3D1&amp;thea=
ter" title=3D"this thread" target=3D"_blank">this thread</a> on our Faceboo=
k page.</p>

<p>Once you're a proficient programmer, you may want to have a source of kn=
owledge through which you can quickly find the answers to emerging question=
s, or just fill in the gaps in your memory. Instead of a handbook, you=E2=
=80=99ll want a book that briefly describes the language standards - everyt=
hing that=E2=80=99s really important and nothing more. You need what is cal=
led a report (permanently improved and updated), published by the <b>ISO st=
andardization committee</b>. You can find the most recent version of the st=
andard at <a href=3D"https://isocpp.org/std" title=3D"https://isocpp.org/st=
d" target=3D"_blank" style=3D"color: #00bceb;">https://isocpp.org/std</a>.<=
/p>

<p>But hey, it=E2=80=99s too soon for that. Look into that when you finish =
this course. No sooner.</p>
